      {
        "heading": "Protocol Anomalies: When Traffic Uses the Wrong Door",
        "content": "A recurring, high-value detection idea cuts across every protocol in this lesson: attackers frequently make traffic **use a protocol or port in a way that doesn't match what's supposed to be there**, and spotting that mismatch is one of the most reliable ways to find covert channels and evasion. The principle is simple, *know what normal looks like for a port and a protocol, and treat deviations as suspicious*, but applying it well is a genuine skill.\n\nThe mismatches come in a few recognisable shapes:\n\n- **Port–service mismatch.** Each well-known port has an expected protocol: 443 should carry HTTPS, 22 SSH, 53 DNS. When the *traffic* on a port isn't the protocol that port is for (non-TLS data on 443, an SSH handshake on 443, a raw tunnel on 53) that discrepancy is a strong evasion signal. Attackers deliberately run C2 on 443 precisely because it is almost always allowed, but the *content* often betrays that it isn't really web traffic.\n- **Right protocol, wrong role.** A protocol behaving in a way its role doesn't call for: a workstation *serving* SMB to many peers (it should be a client, not a server), a host suddenly acting as a DNS resolver, or an internal machine making the kind of outbound connections only a server should. The protocol is legitimate; the *direction and role* are wrong.\n- **Protocol on a non-standard port.** The inverse of the first case: a real service moved somewhere unexpected, like SSH on port 2222 or RDP on a high port, to dodge simple port-based rules. The protocol is recognisable if you inspect the content, even though the port is \"wrong.\"\n- **Volume and rhythm anomalies.** DNS that carries far more data than name resolution needs, or ICMP (ping) traffic carrying a payload. Both are legitimate protocols bent into covert tunnels, detectable because their *shape* doesn't match their normal purpose.\n\nThe unifying detection logic, which ties back to the whole course, is **baseline and deviation**: you cannot recognise an anomalous protocol without knowing what the normal protocol behaviour on that port, for that host, looks like. This is also why modern detection increasingly relies on **deep packet inspection and protocol analysis** (tools like Zeek that identify the *actual* protocol regardless of port) rather than trusting the port number, because the port is a *claim* about what the traffic is, and attackers lie about it constantly. The takeaway for the analyst: **the port tells you what the traffic is supposed to be; the content and the behaviour tell you what it actually is**, and the gap between those two is where a great deal of evasion hides in plain sight."
      }
