      "Identity protocols carry the credential attacks: LDAP recon shows as broad subtree searches (Zeek ldap_search.log, DC Event 1644 once diagnostics are on), Kerberoasting as bursts of 4769 with TicketEncryptionType 0x17, AS-REP roasting as 4768 with PreAuthType 0, RDP as 4624 LogonType 10 plus 1149/21/24/25, and WinRM as POSTs to /wsman on 5985 with wsmprovhost.exe spawning commands on the target.",
      "Encryption removes payload, not all evidence: DoH hides DNS inside HTTPS to public resolvers, QUIC moves HTTP/3 to UDP 443 past TCP-based inspection, and TLS metadata (missing SNI, self-signed or new certificates in x509.log, odd ALPN) remains huntable, with JA4 more stable than JA3 and ECH shrinking SNI's value.",
      "Placing a protocol on its layer tells you which device can see it: a layer 3-4 flow log proves a connection happened, never the domain or URL inside it; and protocol anomalies (port-service mismatch like non-TLS on 443, right-protocol-wrong-role, protocol on a non-standard port, volume/rhythm) are high-value tells because the port is only a CLAIM. Content and behaviour (deep packet inspection / Zeek) reveal what the traffic actually is."
